const e=JSON.parse(`[{"p":"christologyIslam","t":"The Eternal Deity of Jesus Christ","x":"The Eternal Deity of Jesus Christ A source-aware study of John 1:1–14, John 20:28, Hebrews 1:8–12 and Qur’an 5:72. Greek and Arabic analysis, context, Muslim objections, responses, and presuppositional method."},{"p":"christologyIslam","t":"Thesis and confessional framework","x":"Thesis and confessional framework One divine essence; three persons; one incarnate Son The Christian claim is not that Jesus is a second deity beside the Father. The eternal Son shares the one undivided divine essence with the Father and the Holy Spirit. The Son assumed a complete human nature without ceasing to be God. The Second London Baptist Confession (1689), chapters 2 and 8, distinguishes the persons of the Trinity and confesses the one Christ as truly God and truly man. Scripture is the final authority; the confession summarizes its teaching. Our central question is whether the New Testament places Jesus on the Creator side of the Creator–creature distinction, and whether Qur’an 5:72 denies the same Christological claim. Editorial analysis, not a substitute for reading the full primary texts in context. Greek grammar and textual questions are explained at a research-guide level."},{"p":"christologyIslam","t":"Personal research notes","x":"Personal research notes Saved in this browser on this device; not synced across devices."},{"p":"trinity4171","t":"Qur’an 4:171 — “Do Not Say Three”","x":"Qur’an 4:171 — “Do Not Say Three” Arabic grammar, classical Sunni tafsir, Greek exegesis of Matthew 28:19, John 1 and 2 Corinthians 13:14, confessional Reformed responses and presuppositional analysis."},{"p":"trinity4171","t":"Thesis and textual context","x":"Thesis and textual context Qur’an 4:171 and the identity of God Qur’an 4:171 addresses the People of the Book, identifies Jesus as the Messiah and messenger of Allah, calls him His word conveyed to Mary and a spirit from Him, prohibits saying “Three,” affirms one God, and rejects divine offspring. These claims form a connected argument, not a detached numerical objection. Classical Reformed Baptist theology affirms one undivided divine essence subsisting eternally in Father, Son and Holy Spirit. It rejects three gods, three parts of God, and the claim that Mary is a person of the Trinity. The real disagreement concerns whether the one God has eternally distinct personal relations and whether the Son is truly divine. The apologetic task is to represent the Qur’anic objection fairly, interpret each source in context, and examine the authority claims of Scripture and the Qur’an. A Muslim may understand Nicene theology accurately and nevertheless reject it. Study-guide analysis. Verify citations and disputed grammatical or historical claims against the primary sources."},{"p":"trinity4171","t":"Personal research notes","x":"Personal research notes Stored locally in this browser, not synchronized across devices."},{"p":"quran","t":"Qur'anic Textual Research","x":"Qur'anic Textual Research Read Qur’anic passages in English using the public-domain 1930 Pickthall translation; optional Arabic is available for language-specific analysis. Curated findings are research leads, not an exhaustive critical apparatus."},{"p":"quran","t":"Corpus and translation status","x":"Corpus and translation status Ready: 6,236 verses across all 114 surahs · Tanzil Qur’an Text (Simple, Version 1.1). Arabic text is searchable offline after the app loads. English translation: English translation unavailable: TypeError: Failed to fetch. Internet connection is required for the first load; retry by refreshing. No partial text will be misrepresented as complete. Bundled source: Tanzil Project, Qur’an Text (Simple, Version 1.1), © 2007–2026, CC BY 3.0. The original Arabic text is included verbatim with its full copyright notice. Search normalization is performed separately and does not alter the source. View original source and license."},{"p":"quran","t":"Find a verse by reference","x":"Find a verse by reference Search references or English keywords. The English translation requires an internet connection on first use and is cached when browser storage permits. Open “Corpus and translation status” above if results are unavailable."},{"p":"hadith","t":"Hadith Research","x":"Hadith Research The complete Sahih al-Bukhari in Arabic with the English translation of M. Muhsin Khan, studied from a confessional Reformed Baptist position. The collection is built into the app and works offline."},{"p":"hadith","t":"Why a Reformed Christian studies the hadith","x":"Why a Reformed Christian studies the hadith Scripture alone is God’s Word and the final authority (1689 Confession 1.1, 1.10). The hadith are studied as evidence of what Islam claims, not as revelation. Christians are called to give a reason for their hope (1 Peter 3:15) and to take every thought captive to Christ (2 Corinthians 10:5). That includes testing Islam by its own sources and showing where they fail. Why the text is given word for word. The ninth commandment forbids misrepresenting a neighbor’s words, and a critique of a misquoted source proves nothing. The reports below are the Muhsin Khan translation as published, unaltered. Notes and cross-references sit beside the text, never inside it."},{"p":"hadith","t":"Find a report","x":"Find a report 7,580 of 7,580 reports. Showing up to 25 per page."},{"p":"hadith","t":"About this edition","x":"About this edition Numbering follows sunnah.com (Bukhari 1–7563), with book and hadith-in-book references. The older Khan Volume:Book:Number from your IslamHouse PDF is shown wherever the report could be matched by text (6,465 reports). Lettered numbers such as 1:8:385l are kept as separate reports. The IslamHouse PDF has no Volume 9, Book 83 and stops at Volume 5, Book 59, No. 411. Those reports are included here from the full collection, under their Bukhari numbers only. Source: Sahih al-Bukhari, Arabic text and Khan translation with sunnah.com numbering, from the public-domain fawazahmed0/hadith-api dataset. Verify any quotation against sunnah.com before publishing it."},{"p":"hadith","t":"Other Sunni hadith collections","x":"Other Sunni hadith collections These collections remain external references until separate source files are supplied and verified."},{"p":"scripture","t":"Scripture Research","x":"Scripture Research Navigate all 66 biblical books, locate references, save verse-level notes, and explore connections to Qur'anic studies. The supplied NASB document is a research source; its full text is not redistributed in this public application."},{"p":"scripture","t":"Scripture is the supreme judge","x":"Scripture is the supreme judge The Holy Scripture is “the only sufficient, certain, and infallible rule of all saving knowledge, faith, and obedience” (1689 Confession 1.1). Every other source in this app, including creeds, councils, scholars and other religions’ texts, is examined by it (1.10). Its meaning is found by comparing Scripture with Scripture (1.9), and its full persuasion comes from the Holy Spirit’s inward witness (1.5)."},{"p":"scripture","t":"Genesis 1:1","x":"Genesis 1:1 Use the offline Bible panel below to read imported Scripture text."},{"p":"scripture","t":"Bible–Qur'an research connections","x":"Bible–Qur'an research connections Studies mentioning Genesis: 2. Select Qur'anic Research from the sidebar to read the complete comparison studies. Creation in six days and the sequence in 41:9–12 Abraham and the binding of his son"},{"p":"sources","t":"Sources & Verification","x":"Sources & Verification What has been checked, what still needs checking, named Muslim and Jewish sources for each account, and a reading list. Scripture stays the standard; manuscripts show preservation (1689 1.8) and do not rank above inspiration (2 Timothy 3:16). CONFIRMED · Bible Hub NASB links (biblehub.com/nasb/book/c-v.htm)Checked live for John 3, John 3:16 and 1 Samuel 1:1 on 21 Nov 2026. CONFIRMED · Offline Bible text: BSB, KJV, WLC Hebrew, SBLGNT GreekLoaded from published open-licence editions; all 66 books present. CONFIRMED · Pickthall Qur’an English and Tanzil ArabicPublic sources; Arabic is Tanzil Simple v1.1. VERIFY · Manuscript dates and holdings in the timelineWritten from standard references; check against museum catalogues before citing. VERIFY · Tafsir and Hadith references on Muslim positionsBook and surah references are given; wording of individual tafsir opinions should be checked in the work itself. VERIFY · Rabbinic and Jewish parallelsTractate and chapter references are standard; check on Sefaria.org. VERIFY · 1689 Confession text with proof textsEmbedded from the PDF you supplied (32 chapters, 160 paragraphs, proofs included). Spot-check against a printed edition before formal quotation. VERIFY · Contradiction cards (18) on the Torah and Qur’anic Accounts pageBible and Qur’an wording is exact (KJV and Pickthall, Qur’an Arabic from Tanzil). Tafsir excerpts were seen on Quran.com, al-Islam.org and IslamQA; each Muslim explanation is marked checked, secondary or verify. Ṭabarī was not read directly, and the Jewish sources (Genesis Rabbah, Pirke de-Rabbi Eliezer, Tanḥuma, the Testament of Solomon) are marked verify."},{"p":"sources","t":"VERIFY · Roman Catholicism rows (21 plus 4 practices) on the Roman Catholicism p","x":"VERIFY · Roman Catholicism rows (21 plus 4 practices) on the Roman Catholicism pageCatechism (including 85–86, 1440–1497, 1536–1553), Trent Sessions 4, 6, 7, 13, 14, 22, 23 and 25, Nicaea II, Pastor Aeternus ch. 4, Dei Verbum 10, the Marian definitions and Cyprian Unity 4 were read at source. Trent canon 32 was read only in part. Athanasius, Jerome, Augustine and Irenaeus on these points came through excerpts or secondary summaries. Denzinger links are unverified leads. VERIFY · Latter-day Saints page (19 entries)Read at source: Ensign April 1971 King Follett text, Premortality, True to the Faith plan of salvation, Baptisms for the Dead, Apostasy and Restoration pages. All other entries cite the church’s manuals and scriptures by name, and their links were not opened in this pass. VERIFY · Jehovah’s Witnesses page (18 entries)Read at source: jw.org pages on Michael, John 1:1, the holy spirit, Jesus’ body after the resurrection, blood, and the Watchtower on disfellowshipping. Entries on the soul, hell, 144,000, 1914, the organization, predictions, holidays, ministry and neutrality are marked verify. VERIFY · Abrogation table and the nine added doctrinesVerse texts are exact (Pickthall and Tanzil). Scholar names, counts, classifications and hadith references come from standard works but are not checked against them. UNCONFIRMED · Khan Bukhari translation and Saheeh International PDFCheck redistribution licence before keeping them on the public site."},{"p":"privacy","t":"Privacy & Deployment","x":"Privacy & Deployment Veritas Reformata is a static Netlify application. Google Drive source files are not included in the deployed bundle. Your notes currently remain in this browser's local storage."},{"p":"privacy","t":"Protect the whole Netlify site","x":"Protect the whole Netlify site In Netlify, open your site's settings and look for visitor access or password protection. Enable Netlify's supported site-level protection if your plan offers it. If not, use a supported external access-control service or a private deployment arrangement. Do not put a password in React code or environment variables exposed to the browser. This app does not claim to authenticate users. Until hosting-level access controls are configured, anyone with your public site URL may view the application and its built-in content."},{"p":"privacy","t":"Google Drive permissions","x":"Google Drive permissions Links open in Google Drive and respect your Google account's permissions. Keep source files private. Do not change Drive sharing to “Anyone with the link” just to make them work in Veritas Reformata."},{"p":"privacy","t":"Research notes and backups","x":"Research notes and backups Notes, bookmarks and source annotations are saved in localStorage on this device and browser. They are not synced to Drive or Netlify. Export backups regularly, especially before clearing browser data."},{"p":"privacy","t":"Copyright and licensed collections","x":"Copyright and licensed collections The Ultimate English / Ultimate USB license restricts incorporation into other digital systems. Veritas Reformata includes folder pointers only; no licensed documents or extracted text are packaged in this application. Obtain written permission before embedding the files or their contents."},{"p":"manuscriptCriticism","t":"Manuscript Criticism: Old Testament & Qur’an","x":"Manuscript Criticism: Old Testament & Qur’an How the texts were transmitted, what the manuscripts show, and where scholars disagree, always judged by Scripture. Scripture is inspired by God and is the ultimate, unmoving standard of truth by its own testimony (2 Timothy 3:16; 1689 1.5). The Confession is subordinate to it. Manuscripts show how texts were transmitted; they do not rank above Scripture, and they do not make a text inspired. Every other text, including the Qur’an, is measured by Scripture."},{"p":"manuscriptCriticism","t":"Manuscript timeline","x":"Manuscript timeline Dates are scholarly or scientific ranges and are under discussion; check the cited institutions. Manuscripts show how a text was copied and kept (1689 1.8). They do not decide what is true. Date: about 7th–6th century BC (dating debated) Two small silver scrolls with a form of the priestly blessing (Numbers 6:24–26). Among the earliest known biblical text; some scholars propose a later date. Date: about 3rd century BC–1st century AD Dozens of copies of the books of Moses from the caves near Qumran. More than 600 years before Muhammad; text generally close to the later Hebrew text, with some variants. Open source ↗ Date: about 2nd–1st century BC A fragment with the Ten Commandments and the Shema. Shows liturgical use of Torah passages. Date: begun 3rd century BC; manuscripts 4th century AD onward Greek translation of the Torah, later all of the Old Testament. Independent witness; sometimes preserves different readings. Date: tradition ancient; medieval manuscripts The Torah as preserved by the Samaritans. Thousands of small differences from the Hebrew text, most minor. Date: radiocarbon 568–645 AD (reported) Two parchment leaves with parts of Q 18–20 in Hijazi script. Dating applies to the parchment, not necessarily the writing; ranges are debated. Date: lower text radiocarbon about 578–669 AD (reported) A manuscript with an erased earlier text beneath the visible Qur’an."},{"p":"manuscriptCriticism","t":"The lower text shows some differences in wording and order from the standard tex","x":"The lower text shows some differences in wording and order from the standard text; scholars discuss their significance. Open source ↗ Date: radiocarbon about 649–675 AD (reported; verify) An early Qur’an fragment. Among the earliest dated copies. Date: about 8th–9th century AD (Samarkand radiocarbon 775–995 at 95.4%, another folio 595–855; Topkapı dating verify) Large early codices traditionally linked to the ‘Uthmanic text. The link to ‘Uthman is a tradition. Neither is shown to be a copy he sent; dating is a range and under discussion. Date: about 930 AD Hebrew Bible with Masoretic vowel and accent markings. Partly damaged; key reference for the Masoretic tradition. Open source ↗ Date: 1008–1009 AD Complete Hebrew Bible; basis of modern critical editions. Public-domain text widely available online. Open source ↗"},{"p":"manuscriptCriticism","t":"Old Testament witnesses","x":"Old Testament witnesses Standard Hebrew text of the Old Testament, copied and marked by Jewish scribes. Earliest complete copies: Aleppo Codex (about 930 AD) and Leningrad Codex (1008–1009 AD). The base of most Reformation and modern translations. Hebrew and Aramaic copies from about the 3rd century BC to the 1st century AD, including every Old Testament book except Esther. They show several text forms in the same period, mostly close to the MT. Greek translation, begun in the 3rd century BC for the Torah. Manuscripts include Vaticanus and Sinaiticus (4th century AD). Sometimes reflects a different Hebrew text. The Torah as kept by the Samaritans. Thousands of small differences from the MT, most minor. Syriac, Latin and Aramaic translations from the early centuries AD. Useful for checking how the Hebrew was read. What the Qur’an says about the Torah (taḥrīf)"},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Inerrancy & Textual Criticism","x":"Inerrancy & Textual Criticism Ten evidence-led studies of the Dead Sea Scrolls, biblical manuscripts, archaeology, canon, and Scripture’s authority. Historical evidence, textual reconstruction, and theological conclusions are deliberately distinguished."},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Dead Sea Scrolls","x":"Dead Sea Scrolls Qumran discoveries and Hebrew Bible transmission"},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Historical and textual evidence","x":"Historical and textual evidence The Dead Sea Scrolls, discovered beginning in 1947, include biblical manuscripts from before the medieval Masoretic codices. They establish that many biblical books circulated centuries before the oldest complete medieval Hebrew Bible manuscripts. They also attest textual plurality: some witnesses are close to the proto-Masoretic tradition, others resemble the Septuagint or Samaritan Pentateuch, and others are non-aligned."},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Key witnesses and artifacts","x":"Key witnesses and artifacts Great Isaiah Scroll (1QIsaᵃ); 1QIsab; 4QSamuel; 4QJeremiah; 4QDeuteronomy"},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Biblical and confessional foundations","x":"Biblical and confessional foundations Isaiah 40:8; Luke 24:44; 2 Timothy 3:16"},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Strong objection and scholarly caution","x":"Strong objection and scholarly caution The Great Isaiah Scroll is a striking witness to long-term textual continuity, but it contains spelling and other variants. Do not claim that every letter is identical to the medieval Masoretic text."},{"p":"textualCriticism","t":"Reformed Baptist apologetic response","x":"Reformed Baptist apologetic response The scrolls document both substantial continuity and real variation. Textual criticism evaluates individual readings; inerrancy concerns the God-given text, not uniformity of every copy. Open primary collection or reference resource ↗"},{"p":"torahQuran","t":"Torah and Qur’anic Accounts","x":"Torah and Qur’anic Accounts Compare the Qur’an’s accounts with the Torah, the Prophets and the Psalms. Each account shows the English text on both sides and the manuscript evidence, always judged by Scripture. Scripture is inspired by God and is the highest and ultimate standard of truth by its own testimony (2 Timothy 3:16; 1689 1.5). Manuscripts do not make it so; they show it has been faithfully preserved (1689 1.8). Everything else — creeds and confessions, manuscripts, scholarship, and every other religious text — is measured by it, never the reverse."},{"p":"torahQuran","t":"The unmoving standard","x":"The unmoving standard The ultimate, unmoving standard. Self-attesting and sufficient. “To the law and to the testimony! If they do not speak according to this word, it is because they have no light” (Isaiah 8:20). See also 2 Timothy 3:16–17 and 2 Peter 1:20–21; 1689 1.1, 1.4–5, 1.10. A subordinate standard. It summarizes what Scripture teaches and has authority only as it agrees with Scripture. Where it differs from Scripture, Scripture wins. The framers themselves invited correction from God’s Word. Evidence about how texts were copied and transmitted. They never decide what is true; they are not the ground of Scripture’s authority. They are used inside the Scripture-first framework, not as a neutral court above it. Examined fairly in their own terms, then measured against Scripture. They are not a second standard and the comparison is not between equals."},{"p":"torahQuran","t":"Cautions that keep the method honest","x":"Cautions that keep the method honest Scripture is inspired by God and is the ultimate standard by its own testimony (2 Timothy 3:16; 1689 1.5). Manuscripts do not make it so; they show it has been faithfully preserved (1689 1.8). The same limit applies to Qur’an manuscripts: early dates do not make a text revelation. State the other view accurately and in a form its adherents would recognize before judging it (Proverbs 18:13; 1 Peter 3:15–16). Hold your own confessional wording loosely enough to be corrected by Scripture."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Logic & Transcendental Apologetics","x":"Logic & Transcendental Apologetics A searchable reference to logical laws, argument forms, and fallacies, plus a substantial study of the transcendental argument for God (TAG). Apply careful reasoning to Scripture, theological disputes, and comparative religion without misrepresenting opponents. Scripture is the final authority; the 1689 Baptist Confession is a subordinate doctrinal standard. Van Til, Bahnsen, and Bavinck are theological resources, not interchangeable authorities. A fallacy must be demonstrated, not merely alleged; a TAG premise must be defended, not merely announced. Plain-language explanations with simple examples. Each idea builds on the one before it. Scripture is the final authority; logic is a gift of God for thinking His thoughts after Him."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"1. What is an argument?","x":"1. What is an argument? In plain words: An argument is a set of reasons that point to a conclusion. Premises are the reasons. The conclusion is what they are meant to support. Example: (1) Anyone who lies is a sinner. (2) Pat lied. (3) Therefore Pat is a sinner. Two premises, one conclusion. Why it matters: apologetics is giving reasons (1 Peter 3:15). Knowing the parts lets you see what is actually being claimed."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"2. Valid versus sound","x":"2. Valid versus sound In plain words: Valid means the conclusion follows. Sound means it follows and the premises are true. Valid but not sound: All cats can fly. Felix is a cat. So Felix can fly. The reasoning is fine, the first premise is false. Sound: All men are mortal. Socrates is a man. So Socrates is mortal. Invalid: If it rains the ground is wet. The ground is wet. So it rained. The conclusion does not have to follow. Why it matters: you can attack an argument at two places: the structure, or a premise. Name which one you are doing."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"3. Three ways of reasoning","x":"3. Three ways of reasoning In plain words: Deduction guarantees; induction makes probable; abduction picks the best explanation. Deduction: all birds have feathers; a sparrow is a bird; so it has feathers. Induction: every sparrow I have seen has feathers; so sparrows probably do. See the Induction tab for strong and weak versions. Abduction: the lights are out and the breaker is tripped; the best explanation is an overload."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"4. Presuppositions","x":"4. Presuppositions In plain words: A presupposition is what you assume before you start reasoning. Everyone has some. A judge presupposes the law; a scientist presupposes the world is orderly. Example: measuring a table with a ruler presupposes the ruler is accurate. If someone says the ruler is the thing in doubt, you cannot settle it with the ruler. Scripture says the starting point matters: “The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge” (Proverbs 1:7); in Christ “are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge” (Colossians 2:3)."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"5. Why no one reasons from neutral ground","x":"5. Why no one reasons from neutral ground In plain words: Presuppositionalists say everyone reasons from some ultimate commitment. If you say “let’s look at the evidence neutrally,” you have already decided what counts as evidence and how to weigh it. Scripture: “Whoever is not with me is against me” (Matthew 12:30); people “suppress the truth” they know (Romans 1:18–21). So the Christian does not set aside Scripture to argue on “common ground.” He argues from Scripture while showing that the other position cannot support what it relies on (2 Corinthians 10:5). Fair caution: this does not mean the other person cannot reason or be right about many facts. Common grace lets them know many true things."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"6. Internal critique","x":"6. Internal critique In plain words: Test a worldview by its own standards. Step 1: state their view in a way they would accept. Step 2: find something the view depends on (logic, moral standards, the reliability of the senses). Step 3: ask whether the view can account for it. Example: “Only what science can measure is real.” Ask: can science measure that claim? If not, the claim fails its own test. Proverbs 26:4–5 shows both moves: do not answer a fool on his terms, and answer a fool on his terms to show where it leads."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"7. The transcendental argument in three steps","x":"7. The transcendental argument in three steps In plain words: It asks: what has to be true for reasoning itself to make sense? Step 1: Pick something everyone relies on: logic, uniform nature, or moral obligation. Step 2: Ask what that thing requires. Logic is universal, unchanging and applies to every mind. Uniformity needs a reason nature stays orderly. Moral law needs an authority above us. Step 3: Show that only the Christian worldview supplies that foundation, and that rival worldviews cannot, by their own account. Simple example: “You use logic to argue against God. Logic is universal and unchanging. On your view of the world, what makes that so?” Honest caution: the argument must be defended, not just announced. The Transcendental argument tab examines objections and responses."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Worked conversations","x":"Worked conversations Short examples of the method in a courteous, Scripture-grounded style (1 Peter 3:15)."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Logic: “I only believe what is logical.”","x":"Logic: “I only believe what is logical.” Skeptic: I believe only what is logical, and God is not logical. Christian: Good. I also care about logic. What is logic, on your view of the world? Method: Step 1: agree on the thing both people rely on, and ask what it is. Skeptic: Rules for correct thinking that work for everyone. Christian: So they are universal and do not change. How does a world of matter in motion, with no mind behind it, produce rules that are universal and unchanging? Method: Step 2: ask what their worldview says about the foundation. Skeptic: They are just conventions we agreed on. Christian: Then a different group could agree to different logic, and your objection to God would depend on a convention. I say logic reflects the unchanging mind of God (Isaiah 55:8–9; 1 Corinthians 14:33). I’m not saying you can’t use logic. I’m asking what justifies it. Method: Step 3: give the Christian account and invite comparison. Stay courteous (1 Peter 3:15)."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Induction: “Science is all I need.”","x":"Induction: “Science is all I need.” Skeptic: I trust science, not Scripture. Science is based on evidence. Christian: I value science too. Science assumes patterns continue: the same drug works next year as it did this year. Why should they? Skeptic: Because they always have. Christian: That uses the past to prove the future will match the past, which is the very point at issue. I say patterns continue because God faithfully upholds His creation (Genesis 8:22; Colossians 1:17). What would your view say? Method: Be fair: many have answers (such as treating uniformity as a basic assumption). Ask whether that answer explains it or only names it."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Morality: “Right and wrong are just opinions.”","x":"Morality: “Right and wrong are just opinions.” Skeptic: Morality is just opinion. Christian: Then is it wrong to torture a child for fun? Skeptic: Yes, obviously. Christian: “Obviously” suggests more than opinion. What makes it wrong for everyone, everywhere? I say God’s character is the standard, and we know it because He made us in His image (Romans 2:14–15). Method: The point is not that skeptics behave badly. It is that their worldview must explain why morality binds."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Glossary","x":"Glossary Reasons (premises) offered to support a conclusion. It is not a quarrel. Example: Premise: All men are mortal. Premise: Socrates is a man. Conclusion: Socrates is mortal. A statement given as a reason for the conclusion. Example: “It is raining” is a premise for “the ground is wet.” The statement the premises are meant to support. Example: “Therefore, bring an umbrella.” If the premises were true, the conclusion would have to be true. Validity is about the structure, not about whether the premises are actually true. Example: All fish can fly. A salmon is a fish. So a salmon can fly. (Valid form, false premise.) A valid argument whose premises are actually true. Example: All men are mortal. Socrates is a man. So Socrates is mortal. Reasoning where a true set of premises guarantees the conclusion. Example: All birds have feathers. A sparrow is a bird. So a sparrow has feathers. Reasoning from observed cases to a general or likely conclusion. The conclusion is probable, not guaranteed. Example: Every dog I have met barks, so this dog probably barks. Inferring the best explanation of the evidence (“inference to the best explanation”). Example: The kitchen floor is wet and the sink is running over, so the sink is probably the source. A thing is what it is. Example: A chair is a chair. Something cannot be true and false in the same way at the same time. Example: The light cannot be fully on and fully off in the same room at the same moment."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"A statement is either true or false; there is no third option.","x":"A statement is either true or false; there is no third option. Example: “The door is open” is either true or false. If P then Q. P. Therefore Q. (Valid) Example: If it rains, the ground is wet. It rains. So the ground is wet. If P then Q. Not Q. Therefore not P. (Valid) Example: If it rained, the ground is wet. The ground is dry. So it did not rain. If P then Q. Q. Therefore P. (Invalid) Example: If it rains, the ground is wet. The ground is wet. So it rained. (A hose could have done it.) If P then Q. Not P. Therefore not Q. (Invalid) Example: If it rains, the ground is wet. It did not rain. So the ground is dry. (A hose could still wet it.) The cases you actually examined. Example: Ten apples taken from one tree. Concluding that a whole group is like the sample. Example: The ten apples were sweet, so the tree’s apples are sweet. The assumption that the future will resemble the past, so patterns we observed continue. Example: We expect bread to nourish tomorrow as it did yesterday. Two things tend to happen together. Example: Ice cream sales and sunburns both rise in summer. One thing produces the other. It needs more than correlation to show. Example: Heating water causes it to boil. Arguing that because two things are alike in relevant ways, they are alike in one more way. Example: Both engines have the same design, so this one probably also overheats on long climbs. A mistake in reasoning that makes an argument weaker than it looks."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"Example: “You can’t trust his math, he’s rude.”","x":"Example: “You can’t trust his math, he’s rude.” Attacking a weak or distorted version of what someone said. Example: “You want a budget, so you want to ruin the project.” Attacking the person instead of the argument. Example: “He failed once, so ignore his proposal.” Assuming in the premises what you are trying to prove. (Also called circular reasoning.) Example: “This book is true because it says it is true,” when the claim of truth is what is in question. Offering only two options when more exist. Example: “Either we spend everything or we do nothing.” Using one word with two different meanings in the same argument. Example: “A feather is light; what is light cannot be dark; so a feather cannot be dark.” A starting assumption that shapes how you reason about everything else. Example: Measuring a table with a ruler presupposes the ruler is accurate. The set of basic beliefs through which someone understands reality, knowledge and ethics. Example: Naturalism, Islam and Christianity are three different worldviews. Testing a worldview by its own standards, asking whether it can account for what it relies on. Example: “You say only the observable is real. Is that claim itself observable?” The idea that someone can reason about God from a position that assumes nothing about God. Presuppositionalists deny this is possible (Matthew 12:30; Romans 1:18–21). Example: No one weighs evidence without already having some standard for what counts as evidence."},{"p":"logicArgumentation","t":"The basic opposition between starting from God’s revelation and starting from hu","x":"The basic opposition between starting from God’s revelation and starting from human autonomy. Example: One person starts with “God has spoken,” the other with “I decide what is true.” An argument that asks what must be true for something we all do (reasoning, knowing, moral judgment) to be possible at all. Example: “What must be true for logic to be universal and unchanging?” Treating one’s own reason as the final judge, not subject to God’s word. Example: “I will believe only what my reason can verify on its own terms.”"},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"Apologetics: standards & method","x":"Apologetics: standards & method The doctrinal standards and the research method that govern every Veritas Reformata study. The nine applied briefs on Muhammad and Qur’anic origins are with their studies under Islam & the Qur’an."},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"1. Scripture as final authority","x":"1. Scripture as final authority Scriptural / confessional anchor: 2 Timothy 3:16–17; Isaiah 8:20 Exegesis and canonical context govern doctrinal conclusions; no independent neutral tribunal sits above God."},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"2. Confessional accountability","x":"2. Confessional accountability Scriptural / confessional anchor: 1689 LBCF chapters 1, 2, 8, 11 The confession is a subordinate summary, not a replacement for Scripture."},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"3. Presuppositional method","x":"3. Presuppositional method Scriptural / confessional anchor: Proverbs 1:7; Colossians 2:3–8 Identify ultimate commitments, expose demonstrated tensions, and argue from the Christian worldview."},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"4. Historical and linguistic precision","x":"4. Historical and linguistic precision Scriptural / confessional anchor: Luke 1:1–4; Acts 17:11 Separate primary texts, later traditions, manuscripts, scholarly reconstruction, and theological inference."},{"p":"apologeticsFramework","t":"5. Serious objections and charitable engagement","x":"5. Serious objections and charitable engagement Scriptural / confessional anchor: 1 Peter 3:15; 2 Corinthians 10:4–5 State an informed Muslim objection in its strongest recognizable form, then answer without caricature. Core doctrinal commitments: divine simplicity; one undivided divine essence and three persons; incarnation without change of deity; justification by faith alone; Scripture as final written authority."},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Muhammad & Qur’anic Origins","x":"Muhammad & Qur’anic Origins Nine interconnected studies examining Muhammad’s biography, Qur’anic compilation, manuscripts, recitations, hadith, and competing claims to revelation. Historical evidence, Islamic tradition, and confessional evaluation are kept distinct. Each study now ends with its six-stage biblical brief."},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Pre-Islamic Arabia","x":"Pre-Islamic Arabia Context and sources"},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Historical overview","x":"Historical overview Western Arabia contained polytheistic communities, Jewish groups, and diverse Christian traditions. The Qur’an engages Jewish and Christian themes, but the precise religious landscape of sixth-century Mecca is debated."},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Primary text references","x":"Primary text references Qur’an 2:135; 5:82; 106:1–4"},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Source-critical assessment","x":"Source-critical assessment Distinguish archaeological and external evidence from later literary accounts. Do not assume every feature of later sīrah describes the sixth century."},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Confessional Reformed Baptist response","x":"Confessional Reformed Baptist response The biblical question concerns continuity with earlier revelation, not merely cultural similarities."},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Further research","x":"Further research Fred Donner, Muhammad and the Believers; Robert Hoyland, Seeing Islam as Others Saw It"},{"p":"muhammadQuran","t":"Applied biblical brief (six-stage argument)","x":"Applied biblical brief (six-stage argument) 1. The Islamic claim and the serious objection: Does the emergence of a monotheistic movement establish divine revelation? Islamic texts: Qur’an 53:19–23; 109:1–6 2. Biblical testimony: Deut 6:4; Acts 17:24–31. Read each passage in its own context first. 3. Historical and textual evidence: Distinguish late antique Arabian religious diversity from later narratives; do not assume every pre-Islamic belief is independently verified. 4. Internal critique: ask whether the claim fits its own cited sources and the historical evidence. A difference of conclusion is not by itself a contradiction. 5. Presuppositional response: The Creator is known through general revelation, but saving knowledge is disclosed in Christ; examine rival revelation claims without inferring their truth from historical success. 6. Christian conclusion: state the biblical conclusion positively, name the question of revelatory authority, and invite examination of the cited texts. Research note: this is a curated introductory study, not a complete critical edition of the Qur’an or a comprehensive bibliography. Dates, traditional reports, and contested historical claims require checking against the cited sources."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Word of Faith, NAR & Apostolic Authority","x":"Word of Faith, NAR & Apostolic Authority Seventeen expanded exegetical studies defending historic confessional cessationism and evaluating disputed teachings. Word of Faith, NAR, classical Pentecostalism and orthodox continuationism are distinct. Claims below are representative propositions for examination, not quotations or assertions that every adherent teaches them. Scripture is supreme; the 1689 Confession is subordinate."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Claim under examination","x":"Claim under examination Modern apostles exercise authority equivalent to the Twelve and Paul."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Scriptural references","x":"Scriptural references Acts 1:21–26; 1 Corinthians 9:1; 15:7–9; 2 Corinthians 12:12; Revelation 21:14"},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Contextual biblical refutation","x":"Contextual biblical refutation Acts 1 requires a witness of Jesus’ ministry and resurrection for Judas’s replacement. Paul grounds his exceptional commission in seeing the risen Christ. Revelation depicts the Twelve as foundational. The wider use of apostolos for delegates (Philippians 2:25) does not confer the authority of Christ’s commissioned witnesses."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Apologetic response and qualification","x":"Apologetic response and qualification A missionary may be sent by a church without being a new foundational apostle. Ask what biblical warrant establishes equivalent authority today."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Primary text and exegesis","x":"Primary text and exegesis Acts 1:21–26 Must one be ordained to be a witness with us of his resurrection. — Acts 1:22 (KJV excerpt)"},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Original language and grammar","x":"Original language and grammar μάρτυς (martys), witness; ἀπόστολος (apostolos), sent messenger."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Passage-by-passage explanation","x":"Passage-by-passage explanation Verses 21–22 specify the qualifications for replacing Judas among the Twelve; verses 23–26 record the selection of Matthias. Paul appeals to seeing Jesus in 1 Corinthians 9:1 and describes his appearance as exceptional in 15:8. Galatians 1:1 grounds his commission in Christ rather than human appointment."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Interpretive evaluation","x":"Interpretive evaluation Acts 1 concerns the Twelve specifically, not every use of apostolos. Philippians 2:25 calls Epaphroditus a messenger. Neither broader usage nor missionary commissioning establishes equal revelatory authority."},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"1689 Confessional connection","x":"1689 Confessional connection 26.8–9"},{"p":"wordFaith","t":"Research sources and further reading","x":"Research sources and further reading 1689 London Baptist Confession, chapters 1, 2, 8, 26; Richard B. Gaffin Jr., Perspectives on Pentecost; B. B. Warfield, Counterfeit Miracles; Wayne Grudem, The Gift of Prophecy in the New Testament and Today (comparative continuationist argument); Kenneth E. Hagin, The Believer’s Authority (comparative primary source). These are reading leads, not verified quotations or endorsements. To assess a specific teacher, document the original publication, exact quotation and context before applying the biblical argument."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Creeds, Confessions & Catechisms","x":"Creeds, Confessions & Catechisms The 1689 Second London Baptist Confession is the primary confessional reference. Browse the 32-chapter index, compare traditions, and open sourced primary texts. Scripture remains the final authority. The Confession is a subordinate standard; it must always align with, and be corrected by, Scripture."},{"p":"confessions","t":"1689 London Baptist Confession · 32 chapters","x":"1689 London Baptist Confession · 32 chapters Choose a chapter to locate its original text and proof references in the linked primary source. The headings below are an index, not a substitute for the confessional paragraphs."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Historical document collection","x":"Historical document collection Primary confessional framework for Veritas Reformata; 32 chapters, originally published in 1677 and publicly affirmed in 1689. Early Particular Baptist confession. Historic catechetical instruction associated with Particular Baptists. American Baptist reception and adaptation of the 1689 confession. Presbyterian doctrinal standard; particularly useful for comparing covenant theology and baptism. Question-and-answer summary of Reformed doctrine. Expanded doctrinal and ethical catechism. Congregational confession influential in the development of the 1689 Baptist Confession. One of the Three Forms of Unity. Pastoral catechism arranged around guilt, grace, and gratitude. Synodical response to the Remonstrant controversy. Ancient baptismal confession in its received Western form. Trinitarian and Christological confession; distinguish the councils of 325 and 381. Western creed emphasizing the Trinity and incarnation; not authored by Athanasius. Definition of Christ as one person in two natures."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Doctrinal comparison guide","x":"Doctrinal comparison guide"},{"p":"confessions","t":"Scriptural authority","x":"Scriptural authority 1689: 1689 chapter 1 Compare: Westminster chapter 1; Belgic articles 3–7 Broad agreement on the supreme authority and sufficiency of Scripture; formulations and ecclesial context differ."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Justification","x":"Justification 1689: 1689 chapter 11 Compare: Westminster chapter 11; Heidelberg Q. 59–64 Shared Protestant teaching on justification by faith alone and Christ’s righteousness."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Covenant theology","x":"Covenant theology 1689: 1689 chapter 7 Compare: Westminster chapter 7 Significant differences in the structure and administration of the covenants; compare original wording closely."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Baptism","x":"Baptism 1689: 1689 chapters 28–29 Compare: Westminster chapter 28; Belgic article 34 Confessional Baptist believer’s baptism contrasted with Presbyterian and Continental infant baptism."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Church government","x":"Church government 1689: 1689 chapter 26 Compare: Westminster chapters 25 and 30; Savoy declaration Compare congregational, Presbyterian, and other Reformed polity."},{"p":"confessions","t":"Trinity","x":"Trinity 1689: 1689 chapter 2 Compare: Westminster chapter 2; Nicene and Athanasian Creeds Shared classical Trinitarian orthodoxy. Editorial note: This release provides an indexed source library and editorial comparison guide, not a locally stored verbatim edition or paragraph-by-paragraph alignment. Verify wording, edition, and proof texts against the linked sources."}]`);export{e as default};
